  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "For the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{y}},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(3,1).$",
  "worked_solution": "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{y}}) & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{y}-({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{y})}{{(\\sqrt{y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2y\\cdot \\sqrt{y}-({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}})}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{4{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{y}})}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}}{2y\\sqrt{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(3,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(3,1)} & =\\frac{{3}^{2}+3(1)^{2}}{2(1)\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9+3}{2} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$",
